<div class="wrap">
<header>
  <p class="eyebrow">The agentic bit, plainly</p>
  <h1>Agents Propose, Code Disposes</h1>
  <p class="lede">A language model is used in six places in this pipeline. In every one of them it is allowed to <b>suggest</b> and never to <b>decide</b> &mdash; something dumb and deterministic checks the suggestion against the actual data, and a wrong guess is thrown away rather than trusted.</p>
</header>

<div class="card">''' + gate + '''</div>
<div class="note"><b>That loop is the whole idea.</b> Models are good at guessing what a column probably means, or which of forty datasets is worth a look. They are bad at being reliably right. So you let them guess, and you never let the guess through unchecked.</div>

<p class="side">The six places</p>
<p class="sidesub">What it guesses, and what catches it</p>
<div class="four">
  <div class="job"><div><div class="jt">Finding sources</div><div class="jw">step 0</div></div>
    <div class="pair">
      <div class="p"><span class="plab">guesses</span><div class="ptxt">Search terms for the data catalogues &mdash; what a rail delay archive might be called.</div></div>
      <div class="p chk"><span class="plab">checked by</span><div class="ptxt">The hits either exist and pass the hard gates, or they don&rsquo;t. A hallucinated dataset returns nothing.</div></div>
    </div></div>

  <div class="job"><div><div class="jt">Judging a source</div><div class="jw">step 1</div></div>
    <div class="pair">
      <div class="p"><span class="plab">guesses</span><div class="ptxt">Fills a form: which months does this cover, does it have real times, cancellations, cause codes?</div></div>
      <div class="p chk"><span class="plab">checked by</span><div class="ptxt">Every answer is re-verified against the actual file in the next step. A hopeful &ldquo;yes&rdquo; cannot survive.</div></div>
    </div></div>

  <div class="job"><div><div class="jt">Reading the columns</div><div class="jw">step 3</div></div>
    <div class="pair">
      <div class="p"><span class="plab">guesses</span><div class="ptxt">Which column is the timestamp, which is the delay, which identifies the train.</div></div>
      <div class="p chk"><span class="plab">checked by</span><div class="ptxt">The column must exist, the timestamp must parse, the numbers must be in a plausible range. One retry, then the source is dropped.</div></div>
    </div></div>

  <div class="job"><div><div class="jt">Choosing the history window</div><div class="jw">step 5</div></div>
    <div class="pair">
      <div class="p"><span class="plab">guesses</span><div class="ptxt">How far back each chart should reach &mdash; a week, two months, half a year.</div></div>
      <div class="p chk"><span class="plab">checked by</span><div class="ptxt">Hard arithmetic: the model refuses any series over 4,096 points, and the length must divide by four. Anything else is rejected outright.</div></div>
    </div></div>

  <div class="job"><div><div class="jt">Picking which trains</div><div class="jw">step 6</div></div>
    <div class="pair">
      <div class="p"><span class="plab">guesses</span><div class="ptxt">Which trains make a sensible, varied training set &mdash; a judgement about realism.</div></div>
      <div class="p chk"><span class="plab">checked by</span><div class="ptxt">A fixed quota: at least 25 stations, no more than 30 per station, a spread of train types. Picks that break it are dropped, not negotiated.</div></div>
    </div></div>

  <div class="job"><div><div class="jt">Writing the explanations</div><div class="jw">step 7</div></div>
    <div class="pair">
      <div class="p"><span class="plab">guesses</span><div class="ptxt">Nothing factual. Only how to say it &mdash; turning a stiff template into a readable sentence.</div></div>
      <div class="p chk"><span class="plab">checked by</span><div class="ptxt">The blanks must all still be there afterwards. See below &mdash; this one is different in kind.</div></div>
    </div></div>
</div>

<p class="side">The one worth understanding</p>
<p class="sidesub">The writer never sees a number</p>
<p class="intro">Everywhere else we check the answer afterwards. Here we do something better: we make the mistake <b>impossible</b> rather than detectable. The model that writes the explanations is never shown a single figure.</p>
<div class="demo">
  <span class="lbl">1 &middot; what the model is given</span>
  &ldquo;Delay on this line has run <span class="blank">{mean}</span> minutes over the past <span class="blank">{window}</span>, against <span class="blank">{prior}</span> before, and <span class="blank">{share}</span> of it carries <span class="blank">{cause}</span>.&rdquo;
  <div class="step">
  <span class="lbl">2 &middot; what it hands back &mdash; blanks intact</span>
  &ldquo;Trains on this line have been running <span class="blank">{mean}</span> minutes late over the last <span class="blank">{window}</span>. That is up from <span class="blank">{prior}</span>, and <span class="blank">{share}</span> of it traces to <span class="blank">{cause}</span>.&rdquo;
  </div>
  <div class="step">
  <span class="lbl">3 &middot; what code fills in, from the database</span>
  &ldquo;Trains on this line have been running <span class="filled">8.4</span> minutes late over the last <span class="filled">30 days</span>. That is up from <span class="filled">3.1</span>, and <span class="filled">61%</span> of it traces to <span class="filled">Bauarbeiten</span>.&rdquo;
  </div>
</div>
<div class="note teal"><b>It cannot invent a statistic, because it is never shown one.</b> This also makes it cheap: we rewrite a few hundred sentence patterns once, not fifteen thousand explanations. The alternative &mdash; letting a model write the numbers and checking them afterwards &mdash; is slower, costs more, and only catches the mistakes you thought to look for.</div>

<p class="side">The honest part</p>
<p class="sidesub">Where this is upside, and where it is load-bearing</p>
<div class="note"><b>Steps 0, 1 and 3 are evidence, not function.</b> We already know which dataset we are using &mdash; an agent &ldquo;discovering&rdquo; it would be theatre. Their real job is to make the evaluation of the alternatives reproducible, so &ldquo;we considered these and rejected them for these reasons&rdquo; is a record rather than a claim.<br><br><b>Steps 5, 6 and 7 are load-bearing</b> and run on every build. And the checking step earns its place regardless: one of these checks is what caught a column that turned out to <em>be</em> the answer we were trying to predict.</div>
</div>
'''
